mary voters who backed the more liberal Hennrich, and how heavily Trump-aligned outside groups invest ahead of the debates.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 80 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Marie Gluesenkamp Perez (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Incumbent Democrat Marie Gluesenkamp Perez faces state Senator John Braun in one of Washington's last swing districts, where the partisan lean runs slightly Republican. She enters with a decisive financial advantage, having raised roughly three times Braun's total, and holds a large cash edge with no outside spending against her so far. A Trump-backed PAC has begun targeting the race, signaling Republican interest as the seat could help decide House control.\n\nRatings mostly place the race in Likely or Lean Democratic territory, though Cook and Inside Elections call it a Tossup. Markets price her chances near 90 percent. A complication is her second-place primary finish and a liberal rival's refusal to endorse her, leaving some Democratic-leaning voters to win back. Polling is sparse and dated, keeping uncertainty elevated, but the overall evidence points to a Democratic edge short of safe.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8833, "margin": 7.32}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8392, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8323, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8357, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8044, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8079, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8641, "margin": 7.0}]}